[
{ type: install
  message: <<EOM
Android Studio runs as a Linux binary on top of the Linux binary
compatibility layer.  Enable it (as root) before the first start:

  # sysrc linux_enable=YES
  # service linux start

This loads the Linux modules and mounts linprocfs, linsysfs, devfs,
fdescfs and tmpfs (/dev/shm) under %%LINUXBASE%%.  Recommended for
correct rendering of X11 shared memory in Linux applications:

  # sysctl kern.ipc.shm_allow_removed=1
  # echo kern.ipc.shm_allow_removed=1 >> /etc/sysctl.conf

Start the IDE with "android-studio" or from the desktop menu.  The
launcher checks the Linux layer and uses the bundled Linux JetBrains
Runtime for both the IDE and Gradle (JAVA_HOME points to it, so
./gradlew in the IDE terminal works too).

Devices:
- The Linux adb from the SDK cannot access USB on FreeBSD.  Replace it
  with the native adb from devel/android-tools (ADB option), after the
  first SDK setup and after every platform-tools update:

  $ cd ~/Android/Sdk/platform-tools
  $ mv adb adb.linux && ln -s %%PREFIX%%/bin/adb adb

  Alternatively, start the IDE with ANDROID_STUDIO_NATIVE_ADB=yes and the
  launcher does this on every start (the original is kept as adb.linux).
  The launcher does not touch the SDK without it.  Your user needs access
  to the USB device, see devfs.rules(5).
- The Android Emulator needs KVM and does not work on FreeBSD.  Use a
  physical device (USB or "adb connect" over Wi-Fi), or an emulator
  running on another machine.

File watching: the bundled fsnotifier needs inotify, which the Linux
layer may not provide.  The IDE then warns that external file changes
sync may be slow; this is harmless.  With the FSNOTIFIER option the
launcher uses the native fsnotifier from java/intellij-fsnotifier.

If your locale is not installed in the Linux base, the launcher falls
back to LC_ALL=C.UTF-8.
EOM
}
]
